, trunks, cords and key palsies", "body": "The brachial plexus is formed by the ventral rami of C5–T1.\n\nRoots → Trunks → Divisions → Cords → Branches.\n\n• Upper trunk: C5–C6 — injury causes Erb's palsy (\"waiter's tip\").\n• Lower trunk: C8–T1 — injury causes Klumpke's palsy (intrinsic hand weakness).\n\nTerminal branches: musculocutaneous, axillary, radial, median and ulnar nerves.\n\nClinical tip: always assess myotomes, dermatomes and reflexes on both sides.", "topicId": "t1", "status": "published", "createdAt": 100, "updatedAt": 100}}, {"col": "resources", "id": "r2", "data": {"type": "summary", "title": "Rotator Cuff: Anatomy & Function", "desc": "S.I.T.S. muscles and rehab focus", "body": "The rotator cuff stabilises the glenohumeral joint.\n\nS.I.T.S. muscles:\n• Supraspinatus — initiates abduction.\n• Infraspinatus — lateral rotation.\n• Teres minor — lateral rotation.\n• Subscapularis — medial rotation.\n\nRehab focus: scapular control, progressive loading, pain-free range.", "topicId": "t1", "status": "published", "createdAt": 101, "updatedAt": 101}}, {"col": "resources", "id": "r3", "data": {"type": "pdf", "title": "Shoulder Complex — Lecture 3", "desc": "Lecture slides", "fileName": "Shoulder_Complex_Lecture3.pdf", "fileSize": 2412000, "fileUrl": "https://example.com/x.pdf", "topicId": "t1", "status": "published", "createdAt": 102, "updatedAt": 102}}, {"col": "resources", "id": "r4", "data": {"type": "quiz", "title": "Upper Limb — Muscles & Nerves", "desc": "5 MCQs on the shoulder and arm", "questions": [{"q": "Which nerve supplies the deltoid muscle?", "opts": ["Axillary nerve", "Radial nerve", "Musculocutaneous nerve", "Suprascapular nerve"], "correct": 0}, {"q": "Which muscle is NOT part of the rotator cuff?", "opts": ["Supraspinatus", "Teres major", "Infraspinatus", "Subscapularis"], "correct": 1}, {"q": "Erb's palsy results from injury to which roots?", "opts": ["C8–T1", "C7", "C5–C6", "T1–T2"], "correct": 2}, {"q": "Carpal tunnel syndrome compresses which nerve?", "opts": ["Ulnar nerve", "Median nerve", "Radial nerve", "Axillary nerve"], "correct": 1}, {"q": "Which muscle initiates shoulder abduction?", "opts": ["Supraspinatus", "Deltoid", "Trapezius", "Serratus anterior"], "correct": 0}], "topicId": "t1", "status": "published", "createdAt": 103, "updatedAt": 103}}, {"col": "resources", "id": "r5", "data": {"type": "summary", "title": "Knee Ligaments", "desc": "ACL, PCL, MCL, LCL and tests", "body": "ACL: Lachman & anterior drawer.\nPCL: posterior drawer.\nMCL: valgus stress.\nLCL: varus stress.", "topicId": "t2", "status": "published", "createdAt": 104, "updatedAt": 104}}, {"col": "resources", "id": "r6", "data": {"type": "quiz", "title": "Lower Limb Quiz", "desc": "Hip and knee basics", "questions": [{"q": "Which nerve supplies the deltoid muscle?", "opts": ["Axillary nerve", "Radial nerve", "Musculocutaneous nerve", "Suprascapular nerve"], "correct": 0}, {"q": "Which muscle is NOT part of the rotator cuff?", "opts": ["Supraspinatus", "Teres major", "Infraspinatus", "Subscapularis"], "correct": 1}, {"q": "Erb's palsy results from injury to which roots?", "opts": ["C8–T1", "C7", "C5–C6", "T1–T2"], "correct": 2}], "topicId": "t2", "status": "published", "createdAt": 105, "updatedAt": 105}}, {"col": "resources", "id": "r7", "data": {"type": "summary", "title": "Muscle Contraction", "desc": "Sliding filament theory", "body": "Sliding filament theory: actin and myosin cross-bridges, regulated by calcium.", "topicId": "t4", "status": "published", "createdAt": 106, "updatedAt": 106}}, {"col": "resources", "id": "b1", "data": {"type": "book", "title": "Clinical Anatomy", "desc": "", "author": "Faculty of Physiotherapy", "category": "Anatomy", "edition": "2nd", "cover": "http://127.0.0.1:8790/covers/b1.jpg", "fileName": "Clinical_Anatomy.pdf", "fileSize": 8400000, "fileUrl": "https://example.com/b.pdf", "status": "published", "createdAt": 110, "updatedAt": 110}}, {"col": "resources", "id": "b2", "data": {"type": 